/// How many of `items` fall in one residue class.
///
/// This is the shape `examples/life`'s `population()` has, reduced to the
/// mechanism: a small helper that builds a closure over one captured value in
/// its own body and hands it to `filter`, which invokes that closure once per
/// element. `creatureNamed`, `hash`, `sightings` and `examples/covecheck`'s
/// `runCheck` are the same shape, and none of them writes a closure literal
/// inside a loop -- the loop is `filter`'s own walk, one level down.
fn matching(items: Array<Int>, wanted: Int) -> Int {
  items.filter(fn(item) {
    item % 8 == wanted
  }).length()
}

/// The baseline loop, with one callback invocation per element of a
/// thirty-two element array.
///
/// 62,500 turns of 32 elements is the same 2,000,000 invocations the other
/// mechanism benchmarks make, so this row is read beside `call`: that one
/// enters a declared function 2,000,000 times through the call instruction,
/// and this one enters a closure 2,000,000 times through the higher-order
/// builtin path -- `builtins::walk_with` and `Callable::call_value` -- which
/// is a different route into the same kind of body. The difference between
/// the two rows is what re-entering the evaluator from inside a builtin
/// costs, and that is the only route by which `map`, `filter`, `fold` and
/// `sorted` run any Cove code at all.
///
/// The array is built once, outside the loop, because what is measured is
/// the per-element callback and not the construction of a sequence to walk.
/// It is built by pushing onto a `Vector` and freezing it, which is how
/// `examples/life` builds the `Array<Creature>` its own `filter` walks.
/// What a turn pays besides its thirty-two callbacks is what the real shape
/// pays: one closure built, one `filter` entered, and one array of the four
/// elements it kept.
export fn main() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  var scratch = Vector.of()
  var n = 0
  while n < 32 {
    scratch.push(n)
    n += 1
  }
  let items = scratch.freeze()

  var total = 0
  var round = 0
  while round < 62500 {
    total += matching(items, round % 8)
    round += 1
  }
  assertEqual(total, 250000)?
  Ok(())
}
